        ":question/id": "78f271bc-8d43-5a38-8524-01c52619086f",
        ":question/problem": "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\cos^{3} 2x$.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=u^3,\\,u=\\cos v,\\,v=2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=3u^2,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}=-\\sin v,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\\n&=3u^2\\cdot (-\\sin v)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-6\\cos^{2} 2x\\sin 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
