        ":question/problem": "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{8}(2{x}^{3}).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} ${cos}^{7}(2{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(2{x}^{3})$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{8},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=8{u}^{7},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =8{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8{(\\operatorname{cos}(2{x}^{3}))}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(2{x}^{3}))\\cdot 6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-48{x}^{2}{cos}^{7}(2{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
