        ":question/problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡(2{x}^{2}-1).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v=2{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{x}^{2}-1))^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}-1))\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =12x{sin}^{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
